WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.586
Welcome to Module 3 of the
Attribute-Driven Migration learning path:

2
00:00:04.586 --> 00:00:06.337
keep dynamic rules as code.

3
00:00:06.337 --> 00:00:11.534
This module serves Gate 4, the dynamic
group library and administrative units,

4
00:00:11.534 --> 00:00:15.850
and takes Chapter 04 of Building
the Attribute-Driven Migration.

5
00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:19.636
You'll write each dynamic group
as a reviewed file with tests,

6
00:00:19.636 --> 00:00:24.557
lint it for the guards every user rule
needs, show whom a change adds and removes,

7
00:00:24.557 --> 00:00:27.813
and see how to pause and
resume safely in an incident.

8
00:00:27.813 --> 00:00:32.846
In the lab you'll run Microsoft Learn's
own incident samples against a fake tenant,

9
00:00:32.846 --> 00:00:37.430
lint the program's rule pattern table,
evaluate a rule against a fictional

10
00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:41.487
population, run the pull request
check, and break a rule on purpose.

11
00:00:41.487 --> 00:00:46.177
The code is illustrative: tested against
fixtures and a fake tenant, and the

12
00:00:46.177 --> 00:00:48.975
evaluator follows
Microsoft Learn's page on

13
00:00:48.975 --> 00:00:52.280
dynamic membership rules
but isn't the service.

14
00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:57.180
Why does this matter?

15
00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:02.326
Gate 4 replaces "who sits under the Finance
branch" with "who carries the Finance

16
00:01:02.326 --> 00:01:06.735
code." In this workbook's design,
HR attributes land in coded slots,

17
00:01:06.735 --> 00:01:09.992
and dynamic membership
rules read those slots.

18
00:01:09.992 --> 00:01:12.335
So a dynamic rule is access policy,

19
00:01:12.335 --> 00:01:15.767
and one edit can remove
hundreds of people at once.

20
00:01:15.767 --> 00:01:20.407
That's why the implementation guide asks
for every rule on codes, with guards,

21
00:01:20.407 --> 00:01:24.530
tested against named users, and
shadowed before it grants anything.

22
00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:29.114
In this module you keep the rules as
files in Git, with tests and a linter,

23
00:01:29.114 --> 00:01:32.592
so a rule change gets the same
review as any other code.

24
00:01:32.592 --> 00:01:35.611
And you see what Microsoft
Learn's incident samples do

25
00:01:35.611 --> 00:01:39.000
when a group was paused on
purpose before the incident.

26
00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:45.561
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

27
00:01:45.561 --> 00:01:47.925
which takes about fifty
minutes with the lab.

28
00:01:47.925 --> 00:01:51.367
You'll describe what Learn's
pause and resume samples do well,

29
00:01:51.367 --> 00:01:52.813
and what they leave to you.

30
00:01:52.813 --> 00:01:56.388
You'll write a rule as one
file, with an owner and tests.

31
00:01:56.388 --> 00:01:58.998
You'll explain the guards
the linter enforces,

32
00:01:58.998 --> 00:02:02.088
and why it works on the parsed
rule rather than its text.

33
00:02:02.088 --> 00:02:05.424
You'll run the pull request
check and read its shadow diff.

34
00:02:05.424 --> 00:02:08.505
And you'll explain how the
pause manifest keeps a resume

35
00:02:08.505 --> 00:02:11.250
from turning on a group
that was paused on purpose.

36
00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:13.280
Before you start, finish Module 2,

37
00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:16.963
for how the validation kit's
dynamic group checks are built.

38
00:02:16.963 --> 00:02:22.226
Microsoft Automation Module 2 is optional
background on groups through Graph.

39
00:02:22.226 --> 00:02:27.445
You need PowerShell 7.1 or later, Pester
5, and a Git clone of the repository,

40
00:02:27.445 --> 00:02:31.380
because step four compares
the library with HEAD.

41
00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:39.739
In this workbook's design, every
governed rule uses one of two patterns,

42
00:02:39.739 --> 00:02:44.905
and ANDs two guards at the top: the account
is enabled, and the user type is Member.

43
00:02:44.905 --> 00:02:48.193
So a disabled account and
a guest can never qualify.

44
00:02:48.193 --> 00:02:53.140
The rule reads coded slots: extension
attribute ten for organization,

45
00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:56.893
eleven for worker type,
twelve for position category.

46
00:02:56.893 --> 00:02:58.442
Never display names.

47
00:02:58.442 --> 00:03:00.718
Microsoft Learn sets the stakes.

48
00:03:00.718 --> 00:03:04.467
Its page on managing rules for
dynamic membership groups says

49
00:03:04.467 --> 00:03:08.217
a rule body can't exceed three
thousand seventy-two characters.

50
00:03:08.217 --> 00:03:12.247
String comparisons aren't case
sensitive, so lower-case fin-led

51
00:03:12.247 --> 00:03:16.431
matches the upper-case code, and
the HR side has to keep codes clean.

52
00:03:16.431 --> 00:03:20.075
Its page on dynamic group
processing says changes are usually

53
00:03:20.075 --> 00:03:23.580
applied within a few hours, but
can take more than twenty-four.

54
00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:26.679
And its member of page
says that operator is

55
00:03:26.679 --> 00:03:29.692
a preview that ends on
November third, 2026.

56
00:03:29.692 --> 00:03:34.592
The validation kit already reads the
rules live, with its dynamic group checks.

57
00:03:34.592 --> 00:03:37.183
This chapter moves the
same checks earlier,

58
00:03:37.183 --> 00:03:39.990
into the pull request that changes a rule.

59
00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:49.280
Microsoft Learn publishes five PowerShell
samples for dynamic membership processing,

60
00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:53.248
for an incident: when a rule change
or a mass update starts moving

61
00:03:53.248 --> 00:03:57.365
members you didn't intend, pause
processing, then resume it in order.

62
00:03:57.365 --> 00:04:01.264
The repository pins Learn's
pages and runs the scripts,

63
00:04:01.264 --> 00:04:03.690
unmodified, against a fake tenant.

64
00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:07.284
They do a lot well: pause all
skips a group already paused,

65
00:04:07.284 --> 00:04:11.190
and signs in to the chosen cloud
with only the scopes it needs.

66
00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:12.840
But they leave you four things.

67
00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:15.340
Pause all records nothing
about what it paused.

68
00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:18.388
Resume noncritical turns
on every paused group,

69
00:04:18.388 --> 00:04:21.740
including one paused on
purpose before the incident.

70
00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:24.749
An answer the cloud
prompt doesn't recognize,

71
00:04:24.749 --> 00:04:28.003
such as G C C High, selects
the worldwide endpoint.

72
00:04:28.003 --> 00:04:30.690
And nothing enforces a
wait before a resume.

73
00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:33.583
The verdict, which is
this workbook's guidance:

74
00:04:33.583 --> 00:04:36.628
use the samples as the
model, and keep them pinned.

75
00:04:36.628 --> 00:04:39.508
The runbook uses the
manifest-based functions,

76
00:04:39.508 --> 00:04:43.410
because during recovery the
question is "what did we pause?"

77
00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:50.272
Each group is a JSON file
in the rules folder.

78
00:04:50.272 --> 00:04:54.061
Here's one: FIN-LED
everyone but contractors.

79
00:04:54.061 --> 00:04:55.285
It's a user rule.

80
00:04:55.285 --> 00:04:57.960
The owner is the person
who approves changes.

81
00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:02.535
The critical flag marks the groups
an incident responder resumes first.

82
00:05:02.535 --> 00:05:05.323
The membership rule starts
with the two guards.

83
00:05:05.323 --> 00:05:10.822
And the tests name members of a fictional
population, built to exercise each guard:

84
00:05:10.822 --> 00:05:14.865
a disabled employee, a guest
with a copied organization code,

85
00:05:14.865 --> 00:05:18.586
a worker with an empty worker-type
slot, a lower-case code,

86
00:05:18.586 --> 00:05:21.659
a manager and their
reports, and three devices.

87
00:05:21.659 --> 00:05:25.242
The library's six rules are the
program's own rule patterns,

88
00:05:25.242 --> 00:05:26.735
with the guards in place.

89
00:05:26.735 --> 00:05:31.650
A parser follows the grammar on Microsoft
Learn's page on dynamic membership rules,

90
00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:35.497
and an evaluator tests a parsed
rule against each fixture member.

91
00:05:35.497 --> 00:05:38.636
It's for tests and reviews;
it isn't the engine.

92
00:05:38.636 --> 00:05:42.900
It refuses member of and the date
comparisons rather than guess.

93
00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:48.450
Three tools do the governing.

94
00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:53.163
The linter applies eleven rules,
D R oh one to D R eleven.

95
00:05:53.163 --> 00:05:56.441
Two matter most: D R oh
three and oh four require

96
00:05:56.441 --> 00:05:59.462
the guards to be ANDed
at the top of the rule.

97
00:05:59.462 --> 00:06:01.894
A guard inside an or protects nothing,

98
00:06:01.894 --> 00:06:06.363
and a regular expression over the
rule text can't tell the difference.

99
00:06:06.363 --> 00:06:08.687
That's why the linter
works on the parsed tree.

100
00:06:08.687 --> 00:06:13.555
D R ten is a note: not-equals on a
coded slot also admits someone whose

101
00:06:13.555 --> 00:06:18.599
slot is empty, so add a not-null clause
if an empty slot must not qualify.

102
00:06:18.599 --> 00:06:22.553
The pull request check lints
every rule, runs every test,

103
00:06:22.553 --> 00:06:27.538
and for each rule file changed, prints
whom the change adds and removes.

104
00:06:27.538 --> 00:06:28.951
That's the shadow diff.

105
00:06:28.951 --> 00:06:33.825
And the pause manifest records every
collection's state before an incident pause.

106
00:06:33.825 --> 00:06:39.680
The resume touches only what that manifest
paused, and refuses any other I D.

107
00:06:43.430 --> 00:06:44.155
Try it.

108
00:06:44.155 --> 00:06:49.230
Lab 1 runs in the dynamic-rules folder
of the examples, in PowerShell 7.

109
00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:54.063
Step one loads the harness, which
extracts each of Microsoft Learn's sample

110
00:06:54.063 --> 00:06:58.818
scripts from its pinned page and runs
it, unmodified, against a fake tenant.

111
00:06:58.818 --> 00:07:00.530
The tenant has two groups.

112
00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:02.943
FIN-LED all members is on.

113
00:07:02.943 --> 00:07:06.630
Held for review was paused
before the incident, on purpose.

114
00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:08.451
Run Learn's pause all sample,

115
00:07:08.451 --> 00:07:12.093
then its resume noncritical
sample, and read the states.

116
00:07:12.093 --> 00:07:13.267
Both groups are on.

117
00:07:13.267 --> 00:07:17.367
Held for review was paused on
purpose, and the resume turned it on.

118
00:07:17.367 --> 00:07:21.998
Then run the pause again, with G C C
High as the answer to the cloud prompt,

119
00:07:21.998 --> 00:07:24.154
and read the first call's address.

120
00:07:24.154 --> 00:07:28.380
It starts with graph dot microsoft
dot com, the worldwide host.

121
00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:32.792
In a G C C High tenant, that run
would sign in to the wrong cloud.

122
00:07:32.792 --> 00:07:37.560
The slide trims the setup commands;
the module page has them in full.

123
00:07:41.310 --> 00:07:46.010
Step two lints the program's own rule
pattern table, this workbook's design.

124
00:07:46.010 --> 00:07:47.298
Two rows come back.

125
00:07:47.298 --> 00:07:50.820
The Legal employer pattern
is a Finding, D R oh five:

126
00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:54.859
it reads a display-name attribute
instead of a coded slot.

127
00:07:54.859 --> 00:07:56.998
The row exists to show that case.

128
00:07:56.998 --> 00:08:00.111
The Direct reports pattern
is Info, D R oh three:

129
00:08:00.111 --> 00:08:05.765
Microsoft Learn's page on dynamic membership
rules says that rule can't be combined

130
00:08:05.765 --> 00:08:08.305
with any other, so it carries no guards,

131
00:08:08.305 --> 00:08:11.336
and the group must not
grant access on its own.

132
00:08:11.336 --> 00:08:15.750
Step three evaluates a rule
against the fictional population:

133
00:08:15.750 --> 00:08:19.147
the two guards, and the
FIN-LED organization code.

134
00:08:19.147 --> 00:08:25.323
Five members match: u oh one, oh
two, oh six, oh eight and oh nine.

135
00:08:25.323 --> 00:08:28.573
The guards keep out u oh
four, who is disabled,

136
00:08:28.573 --> 00:08:31.372
and u oh five, a guest with a copied code.

137
00:08:31.372 --> 00:08:35.962
Step four runs the pull request
check against HEAD: six rules,

138
00:08:35.962 --> 00:08:40.820
no findings, no warnings, no
changed files, and exit code zero.

139
00:08:44.570 --> 00:08:47.108
Step five breaks a rule on purpose.

140
00:08:47.108 --> 00:08:51.782
You delete the empty-slot clause,
extension attribute eleven not null,

141
00:08:51.782 --> 00:08:56.457
from the FIN-LED everyone but contractors
rule, and run the check again.

142
00:08:56.457 --> 00:08:57.708
Three things catch it.

143
00:08:57.708 --> 00:09:00.574
The linter notes D R
ten: not-equals on the

144
00:09:00.574 --> 00:09:04.483
worker-type slot also admits
users whose slot is empty.

145
00:09:04.483 --> 00:09:07.071
The rule's own test fails, D R eleven:

146
00:09:07.071 --> 00:09:12.157
u oh six should be outside the group,
and the evaluator now says true.

147
00:09:12.157 --> 00:09:15.719
u oh six is the worker with
the empty worker-type slot.

148
00:09:15.719 --> 00:09:20.658
And the shadow diff prints the change:
the file changed, and it adds u oh six.

149
00:09:20.658 --> 00:09:26.421
One finding, one changed file, and exit
code two, so the pull request can't merge.

150
00:09:26.421 --> 00:09:27.745
Put the clause back.

151
00:09:27.745 --> 00:09:30.628
Step six runs the tests: all seventy pass,

152
00:09:30.628 --> 00:09:35.258
fifty-seven for the module and
thirteen that run Learn's samples.

153
00:09:35.258 --> 00:09:38.220
No module, tenant or network needed.

154
00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:45.751
Lab 2 runs the library in a
test tenant, never production,

155
00:09:45.751 --> 00:09:50.107
with fictional users whose slots
match the population fixture.

156
00:09:50.107 --> 00:09:53.115
Sign in to the right cloud
with the least you need:

157
00:09:53.115 --> 00:09:56.046
a Groups Administrator,
in a delegated session.

158
00:09:56.046 --> 00:09:59.720
Create the library's groups
paused, so nothing moves yet.

159
00:09:59.720 --> 00:10:04.250
Compare the offline evaluator with
Graph's beta evaluator for every test,

160
00:10:04.250 --> 00:10:07.220
so you know the evaluator
agrees with the engine.

161
00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:11.498
Then turn processing on and
shadow-diff live with V K D G oh four,

162
00:10:11.498 --> 00:10:15.520
and drill an incident pause
and resume with the manifest.

163
00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:18.395
To back it out, step six
deletes the test groups,

164
00:10:18.395 --> 00:10:22.707
then lists every dynamic group and
administrative unit that isn't on,

165
00:10:22.707 --> 00:10:27.020
to confirm nothing is left paused
that the record didn't pause before.

166
00:10:27.020 --> 00:10:29.170
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

167
00:10:29.170 --> 00:10:32.930
Follow Chapter 04's Lab 2, step by step.

168
00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:38.067
The federal requirement.

169
00:10:38.067 --> 00:10:45.566
The controls are NIST special publication
eight hundred fifty-three controls in its

170
00:10:45.566 --> 00:10:49.896
Moderate baseline: A C two, A
C three, C M three, C M four,

171
00:10:49.896 --> 00:10:54.543
C M three enhancement two, S A
eleven, S R three and A C six.

172
00:10:54.543 --> 00:10:59.268
A rule change is a configuration
change, and its review is the evidence.

173
00:10:59.268 --> 00:11:04.453
The guards keep disabled accounts and guests
out, for account management and access

174
00:11:04.453 --> 00:11:09.342
enforcement; the evidence is the rule
library and its fixture, in the repository.

175
00:11:09.342 --> 00:11:14.446
Every rule passes its tests and each
change's adds and removes are listed, for

176
00:11:14.446 --> 00:11:19.955
change control and impact analysis; that's
the pull request, with its check results.

177
00:11:19.955 --> 00:11:23.718
A broken rule fails the check,
which is the testing of changes.

178
00:11:23.718 --> 00:11:28.704
Learn's samples run unchanged and are
pinned, with their snapshot and tests.

179
00:11:28.704 --> 00:11:32.462
In Lab 2, the incident pause
records each state first,

180
00:11:32.462 --> 00:11:36.918
and the resume touches only what
it paused: the drill manifest.

181
00:11:36.918 --> 00:11:42.043
And the session asks only for what it
needs, shown in the sign-in and audit logs.

182
00:11:42.043 --> 00:11:46.892
The Key Security Indicators come from
FedRAMP's Key Security Indicators list.

183
00:11:46.892 --> 00:11:50.830
Which output supports which
control is this workbook's reading.

184
00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:56.555
Check yourself with two questions.

185
00:11:56.555 --> 00:12:00.155
First: Learn's resume sample
runs after an incident.

186
00:12:00.155 --> 00:12:03.905
What happens to a group that was
paused for review before the incident?

187
00:12:03.905 --> 00:12:06.843
The answer is b: it's
turned on with the rest.

188
00:12:06.843 --> 00:12:10.880
You saw that in step one of the lab,
and it's why the program's runbook

189
00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:14.917
uses the pause manifest, whose
resume touches only what it paused.

190
00:12:14.917 --> 00:12:19.217
Second: why does the linter work on
the parsed rule, not on its text?

191
00:12:19.217 --> 00:12:22.705
The answer is b: a guard
inside an or protects nothing,

192
00:12:22.705 --> 00:12:25.543
and a text search can't
tell the difference.

193
00:12:25.543 --> 00:12:28.317
The guards must be ANDed
at the top of the rule.

194
00:12:28.317 --> 00:12:30.535
The module page has two more questions,

195
00:12:30.535 --> 00:12:34.901
on what the pull request check shows
for a broken rule and which collections

196
00:12:34.901 --> 00:12:39.010
a responder can resume, each linked
to the section that answers it.

197
00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:43.585
To sum up.

198
00:12:43.585 --> 00:12:47.855
You kept the rules as reviewed files
with tests, linted them for the guards,

199
00:12:47.855 --> 00:12:49.745
saw whom a one-clause change adds,

200
00:12:49.745 --> 00:12:53.735
and saw why an incident pause needs
a record of what was paused before.

201
00:12:53.735 --> 00:12:58.298
The evidence an ISSO keeps: the
rule library and its commits,

202
00:12:58.298 --> 00:13:02.210
and each pull request's
check run and check results.

203
00:13:02.210 --> 00:13:05.649
In Lab 2, the Entra audit
log for the paused creation,

204
00:13:05.649 --> 00:13:10.398
the evaluator comparison, the live
shadow diff and the drill manifest.

205
00:13:10.398 --> 00:13:14.748
Before shipping a rule change, work
through the Chapter 04 checklist.

206
00:13:14.748 --> 00:13:18.335
Next is Module 4: move
Group Policy as code.

207
00:13:18.335 --> 00:13:22.071
The organizational unit (OU)
tree scoped Group Policy,

208
00:13:22.071 --> 00:13:25.298
and Group Policy
inheritance retires with it.

209
00:13:25.298 --> 00:13:29.138
You'll turn Group Policy analytics
into a per-setting move plan,

210
00:13:29.138 --> 00:13:32.241
keep settings catalog
policies as reviewed files,

211
00:13:32.241 --> 00:13:36.810
and create only what a dry run showed,
with the least permission that works.
